{
  "dom_id": "step-37588",
  "name": "Introduction",
  "problem": "The **product rule for radicals** states that the square root of a product equals the product of the square roots:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\sqrt{a} \\cdot \\sqrt{b} = \\sqrt{ab}\n\t}\n$$\n\nWe can use the product rule to combine radical expressions. \n\nFor example, let's suppose we want to combine the radicals in the following expression:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{5x} \\cdot \\sqrt{6z}\n$$\n\nTo combine the radicals, we apply the product rule as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5x}\\cdot \\sqrt{6z} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{5x\\cdot 6z} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{30xz} & \\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "worked_solution": "",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
